Chester:

Search E-Bay for DEC products.  For a really cheap but quite functional
Alpha I recommend an AXPCI33 aka "NONAME".  166mhz Alpha processor, but
can be over clocked quite easily to 233 and maybe as high as 300.  I've
seen the mb and processor go for as little as $39, memory is a little
expensive as it uses 72 pin true parity simms, about $50 for a 32mb
stick.  Has onboard SCSI and IDE, has PCI and ISA slots and fits into a
baby AT case.  Uses standard ISA/PCI video card and SVGA monitors.  You
can set up an entire system for around $150 if you already have some
parts, maybe a little more if you have to buy everything.
DEC Multia's are good beginner boxes too, again about $150 for an entire
system.  Only trouble is they are pretty slow, not upgradeable,  run
quite hot and are famous for "Death by Heat".  I have a few of these,
and they are good starter systems, but you'd outgrow it quick.
I'd steer completely away from AXP alpha's, 3000's or 150's or 200's,
aka "Jensen" et. al.  They are not supported very well for Linux.
Stepping up to where the big boys play will cost you a little more.  If
you have a couple of grand to spare I'd give DB Turner at Island
Computers, (http://www.islandco.com) a call.  You can buy fully
configured DEC PWS's in speeds of 433, 500 & 600mhz for around $2000.
Price will depend entirely on what you have put into them.  For what
you'd pay for a new Intel/Windoze box you can have a REAL computer.

Good hunting!

Tom C.


