fuzzyfelt@my-deja.com wrote:

: Did you specify a block size when you built
: the boot disks? I never used to bother, especially with x86 and Sparc,
: but with Alpha I couldn't boot until I specfied bs=72K.

Floppies are pretty unreliable media and they actually seem to be going
worse on average in recent times.  Your boot floppies have to be "bit
perfect" or they will not work.  In a "normal" usage you can get away
often with something worse (faults will be not used, or marked as used
in your FAT tables, or have a letter or two garbled in your text and
maybe this was a typo, ...).  I strongly suspect that this "bs=72K" has
nothing to do with your success but you simply got lucky on n-th try or
maybe indeed you helped to reduce a chance of error by feeding four
tracks at a time and making life easier for a floppy controller.
"Alpha" here was incidental.  Yes, I had incidents when I produced
a suitable floppy only on the second or the third try.  Faulty ones
were going to a garbage.

BTW, when writing floppies with dd I usually use bs=1440k. :-)

  Michal



