With only two computers, get some good ethernet adapter for each and
run applications on each one.  X-window and terminal applications run
just fine over TCP/IP network.  I have one AXPpci 33 with 21066 acting
as a server on my LAN, with most X applications running on it.  X
server and window manager are running on SPARCstation LC which is I
use as my workstation.  Both are running Debian GNU/Linux 2.2.

Actually I am thinking of buying another AXPpci 33 to use as
workstation and spare parts if my server ever decides to start acting
up.  I would probably clock it down to 133 or even 100 MHz if it does
not slow things down much.  It would run cooler and if the graphics
adapter (and driver for it) is good enough, it would show no real
difference from 166 MHz.

Cluster with two nodes would not be really impressive.  Only situation
where that kind of setup would be useful is testing and developing
some cluster dependent code, without having to do any real work yet.

> I was thinking of a setup like this with a shared scsi bus:

> .      | Alpha |-------scsi--------| scsi data disk external
> |-------scsi--------|  Alpha |   

Your ASCII diagram was completely unreadable, try with fixed-width
font and keep line lenght well below 80 characters.  Also start lines
with whitespace, that should tell clients that like to reformat
paragraphs automatically, not to break such parts of text.

> Can this be done?? and how about termination of the scsi bus??  How
> do I need to setup the partitions on the data disk???

SCSI chain should be electrically just happy when you have terminators
on both ends and nowhere else.  Devices might get confused because of
having two initiators on the same chain.  Mounting filesystems as
read-only from disk into two systems at a same time might work.  At
least it should not break anything.  If you want to share filesystems,
try NFS over network.  IP over SCSI is very much experimental and
there is no support for NCR53C810.  Your Alphas are useful once you
get ethernet adapters for them.

