John Beardmore (wookie@wookie.demon.co.uk) wrote:

: So, for non booting disks on an Alpha 2100 server, do I need to use BSD
: labelled partitions starting at cylinder 2, or can I use any old
: partition type starting at the first cylinder ?

The second.  A partitioning type is essential for your firmware to be
able to handle it.  Once you booted Linux will be happy with all these
partition types and some other as well - provided you compiled into your
kernel (possibly as a module) a support for it.  An extra space at the
start is needed for a bootblock so if you do not plan to put it there
you can use it for something else.  OTOH it may be prudent to leave open
a possibility of putting in the future a bootblock on these other disks
as well without a need to repartition.  Can be nice in an emergency
situation.  It is up to you and depend how you plan to use those disks.

As far as other partitioning types go, then in particular a while ago I
was recovering from Linux on Alpha data from old Xenix disks with SysV
partitioning and "divvy" subpartitions and it worked after some hacking
(if you do not know what I am talking about then, believe me, you do not
want to know :-).

  Michal


