chippr@my-deja.com wrote:
: Ok, I've created new kernels a million times on i386 machines but
: I can't seem to do it with my new alpha box.

: I go thru all the normal stuff: make mrproper;make config;make dep ...

: I get linux/arch/alpha/boot/vmlinux.gz

: I move it to /boot saving the old one...

: Since the aboot.conf file has vmlinux.gz in the zero position I just
: try to reboot

: I get into SRM and type "boot dqa0 -fl 0"

You have done these steps correct (I would probably add a new
kernel, under different name and a higher label, to /etc/aboot.conf 
but this is a detail in a sense).

: It starts the boot but then it dies when it starts the uncompression
: of vmlinux.gz and puts me back into aboot interactive mode.

This is hard to tell in blind.  Maybe you misconfigured something,
or some corruption of a binary occured, or you have a compiler/linker
bug, or ...   In principle it should work but some detail may
get you.  Try to recreate arch/alpha/boot/vmlinux.gz and check
that this is indeed the same thing as what you are trying to boot.

BTW - I usually edit old .config file I run 'make oldconfig' if
I want to change something.  Much safer and faster.

'make mrproper' removes .config as well so save it beforehand if
you need/want a reference for the future.

  Michal

