Marble Head (Marble.Head@nospam.guacamole.com) wrote:
: My experience with other "rescue" images is:
: They all have tar, they all have dd, but none of them have dump/restore.

If you want your customized rescue disk it is pretty easy to construct
one using 'busybox' - http://busybox.lineo.com/.  Yes, this does compile
on Alpha although I did not try yet the latest version.  There is no
dump/restore among tools included there but they are simple to add.

Here is a recipe for a simple boot/ramdisk floppy pair.  You create
a kernel for your hardware which supports exactly what you want without
loading any modules and has a bigger default ram disk size - 8 Megs
should be ample but you may go for a bigger one if you really want
and have memory.  You use your kernel as a replacement for one on
a boot floppy; that one from Red Hat will do just fine and it is
a normal ext2 file system on it so you can mount it and read and write
over it to your heart contents.  You may want 'init' of your own.

You create a basic system using busybox stuff compiled with _shared_
libraries and you use that to populate your ramdisk image.  How do do
that is described in 'ramdisk.txt' file from a kernel documentation and
busybox also has documentation which explains how to set 'init' and
associated things.  You also add to this image (only those required by
your binaries) _stripped_ shared libraries and few extra utilities you
want.  Should be enogh of space for 'bash', although you can live with a
simpler shell provided in 'busybox', and still a number of extra
utilities on your own (fdisk, dump, restore, ... ).  After that you
unmount that image, compress with gzip and 'cat' resulting file to
/dev/fd0 with a future ramdisk floppy in it.  Barring bad sectors on
floppies (happens quite frequently and retry with another floppy if this
is the case) you should end up with a pair of floppies which can do any
rescue job.  Just boot with "load_ramdisk=1".

It should be clear that such pair can be also used to break into any
machine (not even running Linux at all) on which it can be used.  But
this is consistent with an old adage "if your computer is not physically
secure then it is not secure - period".

Making even richer environment on a ramdisk with a help of a CD
is left as an exercise to the reader. :-)

  Michal



