Michael, have a look at this article posted here recently by Martin 
Ostermann:

FORWARDED ARTICLE:

>Message-ID: <8n8a4l$h7u$1@zruty.comnets.rwth-aachen.de>

In article <39976138.CAF947ED@md.prestige.net>,
	Jack Lowry <jrlowry@md.prestige.net> writes:
> I am looking for sugestions for a 100 Mbit network card that is known to
> work in a Jensen.
[..]
> The 3c597 driver seems to accept the card but does not recieve
> interrupts and sees the MAC
> address as all 1's.

The problem is, that EISA cards don't get initalized, even if 
they were configured with the ECU. I suppose, usually Digital Unix
reads the NVRAM information stored by the ECU to initalize the
cards by itself (rather than by the SRM), but Linux doesn't support
this.

So I once patched the 3c95x driver to initalize the card by itself,
but it still didn't work. It appeared as if still no interrupts 
arrived. I then gave up.

Martin
