Martin Sinot wrote:

> Marble Head <Marble.Head@nospam.guacamole.com> wrote in message
> news:3A0719C8.C91E385@nospam.guacamole.com...
> > I have edited the /etc/securetty file on a RedHat 6.2 system, but I
> > still have a "Login incorrect" message displayed, for only root.
> > I added to the file,
> > pts/0
> > pts/1
> > ...
> > pts/9
> > and all root logins through telnet are failed.
> >
> > Any help?
> >
>
> You must add the terminals for which you want to allow root access to
> /etc/securetty. So you have the right file. Only these terminals are
> probably not
> called pts/0, pts/1, ... (at least when I try this, they look like ttyp0,
> ttyp1, ...).
> To find out what you need to add, login as a common user and type
> "who am i" (without the quotes). This tells you what terminal you are
> currently
> logged on. Then you add this terminal and maybe similar looking terminals
> to /etc/securetty.
>
> Note that it is not recommended to telnet directly to root, for security
> reasons.
> It is better to login to a common user and then su to root.
>
> --
> Martin Sinot
> martin@nospam.spase.nl




