I bought a PC164 motherboard with 366mhz 21164 on eBay about a year
or two ago, and it worked pretty well with a SB16 (soundcard),
SMC Tulip-based ethernet, a S3 968-based generic VGA card, and a
NCR 53C825 wide SCSI card.

After a few months of trouble-free operation, the board started to
get screwy. The main symptom is that on boot-up, the ARC console stops
at "initializing device drivers...". It should proceed right to 
"NCR810 ok", but instead it just sits at the "initializing..." message.

When I removed the SCSI card everything worked fine; unfortunately,
this is the only NCR-based SCSI card I have (and it is my understanding
that only NCR-based cards will work for booting on PC164's). About
one time in 5 tries the system boots OK, recognizing and initializing the
SCSI card. Has anyone else had their PC164 boards get flaky after a while?

Otherwise I'm pleased as punch with the system; it's just as quick as
the Pentium II 400 right next to it--that was made about three years
after the Alpha :).

Thanks for anyone's input,
Ryan




