Marble Head writes:

> If you want to add hardware, how can you know what will work and where
> to get it?

<URL:http://talisman.alphalinux.org/ALOHcl/> should be a good place to
start from.  Also check out the documentation of your specific Linux
(or BSD) distribution.  Debian/Alpha does not seem to have hardware
compatibility list, or then I have just missed it.

> This time, I want to add an ethernet card to an old alphastation.

That should be fairly simple, as long as device driver in the kernel
is not broken.  I have been using DE100 ISA cards on my AXPpci 33 (I
got these network interface cards for free).  They seem to work fine,
so I have not been in a hurry to get PCI replacement.  My private LAN
is based on 10 megabit coaxial cable anyway.

If I was getting upgraded right now, I would choose between D-Link
DFE-500 (DECchip 21140/Tulip), 3Com 3C905 (Boomerang/Cyclone), SMC
EtherPower PCI (DECchip 21040) and SMC EtherPower II PCI
(83c170/epic100).  Maybe some others would be acceptable too, try
before you buy.

Any "NE2000 compatible" card would be out of the question.

Take a look at "Linux Ethernet HOWTO" on nearest mirror of Linux
Documentation Project site.

> I have some cards for i386, but they seem to be incompatible.  (srm
> sho dev doesn't show anything about it.)

It does not matter as long as you are not trying to boot directly from
network without using local disks.

